An optimal and elegant way to solve this problem is using dynamic programming with bitmask (or ternary mask).

Since each of the `numSlots` slots can hold at most 2 numbers, each slot can be in one of 3 states: holding 0, 1, or 2 numbers. With `numSlots <= 9`, the total number of states is at most $3^9 = 19,683$.

We can use a base-3 representation where the $k$-th ternary digit represents the number of elements currently placed in slot $k + 1$. Because adding an element to a slot strictly increases the integer value of the ternary mask (without carry, since the digit is strictly less than 2), iterating through the masks from $0$ to $3^{\text{numSlots}} - 1$ naturally satisfies the topological ordering of the DP.

### C# Source Code

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution {
    public int MaximumANDSum(int[] nums, int numSlots) {
        int n = nums.Length;
        int[] pow3 = new int[numSlots + 1];
        pow3[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < numSlots; i++) {
            pow3[i + 1] = pow3[i] * 3;
        }

        int maxState = pow3[numSlots];
        int[] dp = new int[maxState];
        int[] count = new int[maxState];
        Array.Fill(dp, -1);
        dp[0] = 0;

        int ans = 0;

        for (int mask = 0; mask < maxState; mask++) {
            if (dp[mask] < 0) continue;

            int c = count[mask];
            if (c == n) {
                ans = Math.Max(ans, dp[mask]);
                continue;
            }

            int currentNum = nums[c];
            int temp = mask;

            for (int k = 0; k < numSlots; k++) {
                int slotCount = temp % 3;
                temp /= 3;

                if (slotCount < 2) {
                    int nextMask = mask + pow3[k];
                    count[nextMask] = c + 1;
                    int gain = currentNum & (k + 1);
                    if (dp[mask] + gain > dp[nextMask]) {
                        dp[nextMask] = dp[mask] + gain;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
```

An optimal and elegant approach to solve this problem:

### Method Explanation

We have two operations:
1. `"00"` $\to$ `"10"` (reduces the count of `'0'`s by 1 and introduces a `'1'`)
2. `"10"` $\to$ `"01"` (moves a `'0'` to the left past a `'1'`)

Notice that operation 2 allows any `'0'` appearing after the first `'0'` to move leftwards until all `'0'`s form a contiguous block. 

Once all $k$ zeros are grouped together starting at the index of the first zero (`firstZero`), we can repeatedly apply operation 1 (`"00"` $\to$ `"10"`). Each application converts two adjacent `'0'`s into `"10"`. Doing this across all $k$ zeros results in $k - 1$ ones followed by a single zero.

Since we cannot eliminate the final zero (operation 1 always leaves one zero, and operation 2 preserves the count of zeros), the maximum string achievable will have:
- Exactly one `'0'`, placed as far right as possible.
- The maximum number of leading `'1'`s possible is `firstZero + (k - 1)`.
- Therefore, the single `'0'` will be located at index `firstZero + k - 1`, and all other positions will be `'1'`.

If the string contains no zeros, it cannot be changed and is already maximal.

### C# Source Code

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public string MaximumBinaryString(string binary) {
        int firstZero = binary.IndexOf('0');
        if (firstZero == -1) {
            return binary;
        }

        int zeroCount = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < binary.Length; i++) {
            if (binary[i] == '0') {
                zeroCount++;
            }
        }

        char[] result = new char[binary.Length];
        Array.Fill(result, '1');
        result[firstZero + zeroCount - 1] = '0';

        return new string(result);
    }
}
