An optimal and elegant way to solve this problem is using Dijkstra's algorithm.

### Method Explanation
We want to find the path from city `0` to city `n - 1` that minimizes the total passing fee such that the total travel time is at most `maxTime`.

We can use a priority queue (min-heap) prioritizing states by `cost` (total passing fees). The state in our priority queue will be `(cost, time, city)`.
Because the passing fees are all positive, Dijkstra's algorithm pops states in non-decreasing order of `cost`. 

To prune sub-optimal paths:
- We can maintain an array `minTime[u]`, which tracks the minimum time required to reach city `u` seen so far.
- When considering moving to a neighbor `v` with a new arrival time `newTime`, if `newTime >= minTime[v]`, we can skip it. This is because any previously considered path to `v` arrived with less or equal cost (due to Dijkstra's pop order) and strictly less time, thus dominating the current path.
- When `newTime < minTime[v]`, we update `minTime[v] = newTime` and push the new state to the priority queue.

The first time we pop destination city `n - 1`, the associated `cost` is guaranteed to be the minimum cost to reach it within `maxTime`. If the priority queue becomes empty and city `n - 1` was never reached, we return `-1`.

### C# Source Code

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int MinCost(int maxTime, int[][] edges, int[] passingFees) {
        int n = passingFees.Length;

        // Build adjacency list: node -> list of (neighbor, travelTime)
        List<(int to, int time)>[] graph = new List<(int, int)>[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            graph[i] = new List<(int, int)>();
        }

        foreach (var edge in edges) {
            int u = edge[0];
            int v = edge[1];
            int time = edge[2];
            graph[u].Add((v, time));
            graph[v].Add((u, time));
        }

        // minTime[i] stores the minimum time to reach city i
        int[] minTime = new int[n];
        Array.Fill(minTime, int.MaxValue);

        // PriorityQueue storing (cost, time, city), ordered by cost
        var pq = new PriorityQueue<(int cost, int time, int city), int>();

        pq.Enqueue((passingFees[0], 0, 0), passingFees[0]);
        minTime[0] = 0;

        while (pq.Count > 0) {
            var (cost, time, u) = pq.Dequeue();

            if (u == n - 1) {
                return cost;
            }

            foreach (var (v, travelTime) in graph[u]) {
                int nextTime = time + travelTime;
                if (nextTime <= maxTime && nextTime < minTime[v]) {
                    minTime[v] = nextTime;
                    int nextCost = cost + passingFees[v];
                    pq.Enqueue((nextCost, nextTime, v), nextCost);
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

